@Thalley so this is spilling the beans on the API doc that aleksander will be writing but here is how we see it:

if `CONFIG_BT_GATT_NOTIFY_MULTIPLE=y`:
- both `gatt_notify` AND `gatt_notify_multiple` use the queuing mechanism (1ms delayed work) and try to use the multiple notification PDU wherever supported by the peer. (there are some additional constraints for this, like having the same callback/user_data). `gatt_notify` falls back on single notification PDUs when those conditions are not satisfied, `gatt_notify_multiple` returns an error in that case.

if `CONFIG_BT_GATT_NOTIFY_MULTIPLE=n` :
- `gatt_notify_multiple` isn't implemented.
- `gatt_notify` and `gatt_notify_multiple` use single notifications

So all in all, this keeps the 'magic'/automatic behavior that we have now, it just ensures the multi notification PDUs actually contain multiple PDUs. The `gatt_notify_multiple` function should mostly stay as-is, but I will have to rework the actual work item to convert the multiple PDU into a single if there is only one notification when the timeout expires.

There is an alternative, which is having `gatt_notify_multiple` just be a convenience function: this way it automatically downgrades to single notification PDUs when the peer doesn't support it or when `CONFIG_BT_GATT_NOTIFY_MULTIPLE=n` . 
We (@alwa-nordic and I) thought it'd be good to have `gatt_notify_multiple` only output multiple notification PDUs, in case the application developer really wants to use those. But if you (and @Vudentz ) think the alternative behavior is better, just tell us.

